.th EGREP I 5/31/77
.sh NAME
egrep \*- search a file for lines containing a pattern
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd egrep
[
.bd \*-b
] [
.bd \*-c
] [
.bd \*-f
] [
.bd \*-n
] [
.bd \*-v
] pattern [ file ] ...
.sh DESCRIPTION
.it egrep
searches the input files
(standard input default)
for all lines containing an instance of the
regular expression
.it pattern.
Normally, each line matched
is copied to the standard output.
The
.it pattern
matches a line whenever the line
contains a substring denoted by the
.it pattern.
.s3
The flags modify the normal behavior as follows:
.s3
.lp +4 4
.bd "\*-b	\c"
causes each printed line to be preceded by the block
number on which it was found
.lp +4 4
.bd "\*-c	\c"
causes only a count of matching lines to be printed
.lp +4 4
.bd "\*-f	\c"
causes the regular expression to come from a file named
.it pattern
.lp +4 4
.bd "\*-n	\c"
causes each printed line to be preceded by its relative line
number in the file
.lp +4 4
.bd "\*-v	\c"
causes all lines but those matching the
.it pattern
to be printed
.s3
.i0
In all cases the file name is shown if there is more than one input file.
.s3
.i0
A
.it pattern
is one of the following:
.s3
.lp +3 3
1.	an ordinary character (denoting itself)
.s3
.lp +3 3
2.	a circumflex `^' (denoting the beginning of a line)
.s3
.lp +3 3
3.	a dollar sign `$' (denoting the end of a line)
.s3
.lp +3 3
4.	a period `\fB.\fR' (denoting any character but a newline)
.s3
.lp +3 3
5.	`[' followed by a string of characters followed by `]'
(denoting any character in the string;
if the first character in this string is `^',
the pattern denotes any character except newline
and the characters in the string)
.s3
.lp +3 3
6.	`(' followed by a pattern followed by `)' (denoting
the enclosed pattern)
.s3
.lp +3 3
7.	a pattern followed by `*', or by `+', or by `?'
(denoting zero or more, one or more, or zero or one instances,
respectively, of the preceding pattern)
.s3
.lp +3 3
8.	a pattern followed by a pattern (denoting concatenation
of the two patterns)
.s3
.lp +3 3
9.	a pattern followed by `|' followed by
another pattern (denoting the alternation of the two patterns);
a newline may be used in place of `|'.
.s3
.i0
In parsing a pattern, the rules are applied in the order given.
.s3
A pattern metacharacter can be used as an ordinary character by preceding it
by `\\'.
The metacharacters are:
`^', `$', `\fB.\fR', `[', `]', `*', `+', `?', `(', `)', `\\'.
.s3
Care should be taken when
using the characters
$ * [ ^ | ( ) and \\ in the regular
expression as they are
also meaningful to the Shell.
When
.it pattern
is a regular expression other than a simple
string,
it is generally necessary to enclose the
entire
.it pattern
argument in quotes.
.sh "SEE ALSO"
grep(I), fgrep(I), lex(I), rgrep(I), sed(I), ed(I), sh(I)
.sh BUGS
Lines longer than 512 characters are not printed completely.
